# When the same carbs arrive twice

Log 45 g of carbs in your loop, and have both the loop and a second app upload
to Nocturne, and two records arrive describing one plate of food. Nocturne
notices, groups them, and shows you one meal. This page explains when that
happens, when it deliberately doesn't, and what to do about the leftovers.

The same machinery covers boluses, glucose readings, pump events and the rest —
carbs are just the case people meet first, because carbs are the thing most
likely to be entered by hand in two places.

## Linking, not deleting

Nocturne never destroys a duplicate. Records that describe the same event are
**linked into a group**, one member of the group is marked as the one to show,
and reads quietly skip the others. Everything you uploaded is still in the
database, still attributed to the app that sent it, and still there if you
export.

Within a group, the record shown is the earliest surviving one, with its
identifier breaking ties. That is usually the copy from whichever app got there
first.

## When two records are treated as the same event

There are two rules, tried in order.

### The close rule

Two carb entries are the same event when they are **within 30 seconds** of each
other and their carbs are **within 1 g**. This rule does not care where they came
from: two records that close together, agreeing that closely, are one meal
however they arrived.

### The drift rule

Clocks disagree. A connector reading your pump's raw clock and a connector
reading a corrected one can be minutes apart on the same event, and that gap
grows over time. So when the close rule finds nothing, a second, wider attempt
runs — but only under conditions strict enough that widening the window cannot
merge two genuinely separate meals:

- The two records are **within 10 minutes**.
- Their carbs are **exactly equal**. No tolerance at all.
- Both records **name the app they came from**, and those apps are **different**.
  A record with no known source, or a second record from an app already in the
  group, is refused.
- There is **exactly one** candidate group in range. Two possible matches means
  the record cannot be attributed with confidence, so it isn't.

The drift rule applies to carbs, boluses, BG checks, temp basals, bolus
calculations and pump events. It deliberately does not apply to glucose
readings, notes or state spans, where repeating the same value inside ten
minutes is ordinary rather than suspicious.

<Callout type="info" title="What this means in practice">
  Two apps reporting your 45 g lunch, minutes apart, collapse into one meal. Two
  helpings of 45 g from the same app, half an hour apart, stay two meals — the
  drift rule refuses a second record from an app already in the group, so
  Nocturne never turns your afternoon snack into a copy of your lunch.
</Callout>

## When it runs

Three times, so that a duplicate is caught whichever way it arrives:

- **As records are written.** Linking runs immediately after an upload commits.
  It is best-effort by design: your data is saved first, and a failure to link
  never rejects an upload.
- **Every couple of minutes, in the background.** A reconciliation pass revisits
  recently created links and merges groups that ingest could not have seen —
  most often because two connectors synced out of order, and the second copy
  arrived hours after the first.
- **On demand, as a full pass.** Under **Settings > Data Quality**, the
  **Run Deduplication** action walks your entire history. It reports how many
  records it processed, how many groups it created and how many duplicates it
  found, and it can be cancelled while it runs.

<Screenshot id="deduplication-card" />

The full pass is the one to reach for after a migration, after connecting a
second data source that overlaps with a first, or any time you are looking at
doubled history from before both were set up.

<Callout type="tip" title="After adding an overlapping data source">
  Turning on a second connector that covers ground the first already covered is
  the classic way to acquire duplicates in bulk. Add it, let the first sync
  finish, then run the full pass once. Ongoing duplicates from that point on are
  handled as records arrive.
</Callout>

## What it will not do

Worth knowing so the leftovers make sense:

- **Amounts that disagree by more than a gram, more than 30 seconds apart.**
  Your pump says 45 g and your food app says 47 g, ten minutes apart: not merged.
  The drift rule requires exact equality, and 45 is not 47.
- **Two records with no identifiable source.** A source that names no app cannot
  establish that two records came from different places, so the drift rule
  refuses it.
- **Anything ambiguous.** Where two candidate groups are in range, no merge
  happens. Nocturne would rather show you two records than guess wrong about
  which of them is a copy.

For those, editing or deleting the extra record by hand is the answer, from the
graph or the **Meals** page.

## The other kind of duplicate

Everything above is about _different records_ that turn out to describe one
event. There is a simpler case that never reaches it: the **same app
re-uploading the same record**, after a backfill or a phone restore. Loop, AAPS
and connectors attach a stable sync identifier to what they send, and Nocturne
keys on it — a repeat upload updates the record in place instead of creating a
second one. See [carb entries](/docs/food/carbs) for more on sync identifiers.
